<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Belgium–Luxembourg Economic Union</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1921">1921</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as BLEU</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> Economic Union is a customs and monetary union created by a treaty signed in 1921, after Luxembourg had left the German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zollverein">Zollverein</a>. It abolished customs barriers between the two countries and tied the Luxembourg franc to the Belgian franc, an arrangement that lasted until both adopted the euro. The union later became part of the wider <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benelux">Benelux</a> Economic Union with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/belgium-luxembourg-economic-union.jpg" alt="Belgium–Luxembourg Economic Union" width="320" height="124" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Belsch-L%C3%ABtzebuergesch_Wirtschaftsdelegatioun,_25._Abr%C3%ABll_1935,_A-Z_Nr_18.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The treaty was signed on 25 July 1921, although a referendum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> had first favoured closer ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> rather than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>. It came into force when the Luxembourg Chamber of Deputies ratified it on 22 December 1922. For Luxembourg the union had several advantages over the German customs union. Belgium could change its tariffs or conclude trade treaties only after consulting the Luxembourg government, whereas before 1918 Luxembourg had had to accept German decisions, and only Luxembourg's own customs officers worked in the country, where Prussian officials had once served.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The economic frontier between the two countries was lifted, and in 1929 the Belgian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> francs were fixed at a set parity, revised in 1935 and 1944. The union was run by a board of directors of two Belgian members, one of them the chairman, and one Luxembourg member, and by the Higher Council of the Union, with three Belgian and two Luxembourg members. Until 1999, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-communities">European Community</a> rules required separate figures, international trade statistics were published for the two countries only as one combined entity.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The original treaty ran for fifty years and was later extended, and on 18 December 2002 the two countries signed a new convention. The union has been seen as the forerunner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benelux">Benelux Union</a>, founded with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> as the Benelux Customs Union by the London Customs Convention of 1944. Many of its aims passed to Benelux and later to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>, but it keeps some role in deciding more precise measures between the two states.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Belgium–Luxembourg Economic Union” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the First World War Luxembourg left the Zollverein and formed the <b>Belgium–Luxembourg Economic Union</b> in 1921, tying its franc to the Belgian currency.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-luxembourg">Grand Duchy of Luxembourg · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
